title ALES7 — Lotus's transport play head, read the master forward and stop at the end
prompt Continue the autonomous recur (baton prin recur): next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, self-approve design rounds, send each finished increment to origin and xykj61, STOP only at custody gates.
think Third increment of this recur, after ALES5 (clock) and ALES6 (track table) both landed and pushed. No custody gate in reach — the Lotus ladder is pure local software.
think Lindy-first crux-first: the transport is the most durable of ALES6's three roads — the read side of the whole suite, on which every later playback feature (loop, scrub, monitor) is a differently-moving transport. Record is the Mikrophone, edit is ALES2–6, and this is PLAY.
think Designed lotus/transport.rye: a Transport (a bounded sample position pos), read_block (copy next up-to-out.len samples, advance by exactly that, short at end, zero past it), at_end (pos == master.len exact), seek_ms + elapsed_ms (real time through the ALES5 clock, seek past master refuses PastEnd), read-only over the master.
think The crux property: every block read from the start concatenates back to the master byte-for-byte — no sample skipped or repeated — and the end is a hard wall. Read-only means a replay after a rewind reads identical bytes.
obs GREEN on first build across five blocks. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, all seven prior ALES witnesses re-run GREEN (strictly additive). seek_ms's error union (TransportError || clock.ClockError) coerces cleanly.
obs Honest scope kept explicit: no audio leaves the process — a block is copied samples, not a sound-card write; that real write is the paused hardware round. No custody gate reached.
